.TH NA_WAV_TAP 1 "2026-08-30" "naudio" "naudio manual"
.SH NAME
na_wav_tap \- record what a naudio client receives, as a decodable WAV file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B na_wav_tap
.RB [ \-\-host
.IR H ]
.RB [ \-\-port
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-seconds
.IR S ]
.RB [ \-\-out
.IR F ]
.RB [ \-\-align15 ]
.RB [ \-\-tcp ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B na_wav_tap
connects to a naudio (net-audio protocol) streaming server as an ordinary
client, records the received audio for a fixed length of time, and writes it
as a WAV file.
It drives only the public naudio C ABI, so the file it writes is what any
third-party consumer of the shipped library receives \(em not what an
internal buffer held.
.PP
It needs no audio device of its own, which makes it the natural remote end
of an on-air check: run the server on the machine with the radio, run
.B na_wav_tap
anywhere else, and feed the resulting WAV to a decoder (for a digital mode,
\(lqthe audio survived the trip\(rq becomes objective \(em it either still
decodes to real callsigns or it does not).
.PP
When the negotiated sample rate divides by 12000 (48\ kHz, 24\ kHz and
12\ kHz all do), the output is downmixed to mono at 12000\ Hz \(em the rate
WSJT-X-family decoders read.
Otherwise the file carries the negotiated rate unchanged.
.B na_wav_tap
takes no format flags: it reads the negotiated format back from the library
after connect, so a server provisioned at a lower rate needs no client-side
change.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-host " H"
naudio server to connect to (default 127.0.0.1).
.TP
.BI \-\-port " N"
server port (default 4533).
.TP
.BI \-\-seconds " S"
record length in seconds (default 15, one FT8 period).
.TP
.BI \-\-out " F"
output WAV path (default tap.wav).
.TP
.B \-\-align15
wait for a wall-clock 15-second boundary before recording, so the file
aligns with FT8/FT4 transmit periods.
.TP
.B \-\-tcp
use TCP instead of the default UDP_WAN reliability profile.
This is the discriminating run when a UDP recording comes up short: TCP
retransmits, so link loss shows up as delay rather than absence.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
print usage and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
a WAV file was written.
.TP
.B 1
the connection failed or no audio arrived.
.TP
.B 2
usage error, or the output file could not be written.
.SH EXAMPLES
Record 5 seconds from a server on the local machine:
.PP
.nf
.RS
na_wav_tap \-\-seconds 5 \-\-out hello.wav
.RE
.fi
.PP
Record one aligned FT8 period from the radio machine, for decoding:
.PP
.nf
.RS
na_wav_tap \-\-host radio\-host \-\-port 4533 \-\-seconds 15 \-\-align15 \-\-out period.wav
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR na_audio_daemon (1),
.BR na_hamlib_bridge (1)
.PP
The on-air verification procedure, including the negative control without
which \(lqit decoded\(rq is not yet evidence:
.I docs/on\-air\-verification.md
in the naudio source tree
(https://github.com/KJ5HST\-LABS/naudio).
